> That depends :). JRI can be run with or without event loop. JGR runs it with the event loop which you will likely need if you load any other toolkits (you don't need it for Java UI). For more detailed help, you'll have to share your code with us. That RGtk2 works from JGR is a bit surprising to me (because of the threaded nature of R when run through JRI), but I guess we take what we get :).

Ok, the code is very simple. It uses Romanface which is built on top of 
JRI. You have to add those jars to your lib for Java code to work 
http://code.google.com/p/romanface/downloads/detail?name=romanface-plus-deps-1.1.7.zip

import java.awt.Dimension;
import java.io.IOException;
import org.omancode.r.RFace;
import org.omancode.r.ui.RSwingConsole;

public class NewMain {
     private static RSwingConsole rConsole;
     private static RFace rInterface;

     public static void main(String[] args) {
         rConsole = new RSwingConsole(true);
         rInterface = RFace.getInstance(rConsole);

         rInterface.loadRSupportFunctions();
         rInterface.eval("library(rJava)");
         rInterface.eval("library(JavaGD)");
         rInterface.eval("JavaGD()");
         rInterface.eval("setwd('~')");
         rInterface.eval("library(iFlow)");
     }
}

A window then opens and... it's empty. Same code in JGR works (only 
enter "setwd('~')" and "library(iFlow)" cause everything before is 
already in JGR).
It can be related to RomanFace so maybe I should try with a simple 
Rengine code.
I need Romanface because it has a console in it and it is free. Unlike 
JGR it can be integrated in a java app.
>
