> // JavaGD //engine.eval("library(JavaGD)"); //engine.eval("JavaGD()"); 
> REXP rn = engine.eval("plot(rnorm(12))"); --------------- but 
> uncommenting the calls of : engine.eval("library(JavaGD)"); 
> engine.eval("JavaGD()"); does nothing, the default R device still 
> used, I continue investigations.

Ok, it was fixed here by creating a new Thread and starting JGR in it.
But it may not be possible for you as you have to access your REngine 
out of the thread.

That code works :

import org.rosuda.javaGD.GDInterface;
import org.rosuda.javaGD.JGDBufferedPanel;
import org.rosuda.JGR.JGR;
import org.rosuda.REngine.REXPMismatchException;
import org.rosuda.REngine.REngine;
import org.rosuda.REngine.REngineException;

/**
  *
  * @author dev
  */
public class JgrPilot {

     /**
      * @param args the command line arguments
      */
     public static void main(final String[] args) throws 
REngineException, REXPMismatchException, InterruptedException{
         // TODO code application logic here
         System.out.println("test0");

         Thread t;
         t = new Thread(new Runnable() {
             @Override
             public void run() {
                 JGR jgr = new JGR();
             }
         }
         );
         t.start();
         System.out.println("test1");
         do { Thread.sleep(1000);} while (!JGR.STARTED);
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("a<-1");
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("b<-c(1,2)");
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("library(JavaGD)");
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("JavaGD()");
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("hist(c(1,4,3,7,6,4))");
         JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute("print('I rule the world!')");
     }
}

You may succeed in replacing "JGR jgr = new JGR();" with your REngine 
start instruction, but you'll have to define REngine out of the Thread.
It will have to be a final var (yeah I know, this is painful).
Replace "do { Thread.sleep(1000);} while (!JGR.STARTED);" with  some 
condition that assure you that R session is on.
Replace "JGR.MAINRCONSOLE.execute" with "re.eval" and it might work...

