Simon Urbanek <simon.urbanek <at> r-project.org> writes:

> 
> On Apr 15, 2013, at 8:00 AM, Sebastian wrote:
> 
> > Hi, 
> > I tried JavaGD as R device and it seems great, thanks for this tool.
> > We may like to use JavaGD embedded in an application but I didn't understand
> > well how JavaGD works, you may have more informations about these following
> > questions ?
> > We use org.rosuda.REngine.Rserve.RConnection to connect to RServe and to
> > eval() some R expressions.
> > 
> > - Is there any way to "redirect" the results of eval() calls to an embedded
> > JavaGD component (GDCanvas ?) for drawing directly in the application and
> > how we could do this ?
> 
> Since Rserve is on the server, you can't use JavaGD in the typical sense
as a visual display. There are a few options:
> 
> a) create the plot server-side and push it through Rserve. This is want
most applications do since you have
> full freedom on the Java side. See PlotDemo.java in Rserve examples.
> 
> b) You could create a back-end for JavaGD that doesn't show the display
but instead sends it to the client in
> some encoded form. You could simply serialize the GD objects for example.
> 
> c) use xGD package (https://www.rforge.net/xGD/ ) - it serializes graphics
and there is a Java example
> unserializing it and displaying it. It is slightly older so if you decide
to got that way, let me know and
> I'll see if it needs updates for the latests R.
> 
> > I guess if I only switch to the JavaGD device in our R instance, result will
> > be popup in the default JavaGD frame.
> > I saw a similar problem in this list where the advise was to set the
> > JAVAGD_CLASS_NAME env.var to a custom Java class, but I don't understand
> > where the custom Java class should be located. Actually, I'm also a few
> > confused because I can't find any JavaGD directory in "R/library" after
> > installing the JavaGD package.
> > 
> > - In our application, we'd need to give some semantic to the graphics
> > elements. For example, a plot need to know his value for using it in some
> > menus or for other usages.
> > Could you tell me if some kind of original semantic is sent to the Java2D
> > objects ? (ie : values or text labels for some data)
> 
> Unfortunately I fear you're out of luck. R doesn't provide any semantics
at all with the plots. There is no
> way to know if the object being drawn is a data point, a legend or some
random annontation. You simply get
> "draw line form there to here" with no semantics. Have a look at iPlots
(http://iPlots.org ) if you want
> graphics with semantics.
> 
> Cheers,
> Simon
> 


